{
  "dom_id": "step-q252038",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Let the random variable $Y$ be defined as\n\n$$\nY={Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{40}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${Z}_{i}∼N(0,1)$ for $1\\le i\\le 40.$ Find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\ge y)=0.15.$\n\n*Hint: The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $39$ | $\\,62.428\\,$ | $\\,54.572\\,$ | $\\,50.660\\,$ | $\\,48.126\\,$ | $\\,46.173\\,$ |\n| $40$ | $\\,63.691\\,$ | $\\,55.758\\,$ | $\\,51.805\\,$ | $\\,49.244\\,$ | $\\,47.269\\,$ |\n| $41$ | $\\,64.950\\,$ | $\\,56.942\\,$ | $\\,52.949\\,$ | $\\,50.360\\,$ | $\\,48.363\\,$ |\n\nThe value of $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\ge y)=0.15$ is $y=$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "If ${Z}_{i}∼N(0,1)$ for $1\\le i\\le k$ are independent normal random variables, then the sum\n\n$$\n{Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{k}^{2}\n$$\n\nfollows a ${χ}^{2}(k)$ distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\nY={Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{40}^{2}∼{χ}^{2}(40).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\ge y)=0.15$ This can be found from the given table:\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $39$ | $\\,62.428\\,$ | $\\,54.572\\,$ | $\\,50.660\\,$ | $\\,48.126\\,$ | $\\,46.173\\,$ |\n| $40$ | $\\,63.691\\,$ | $\\,55.758\\,$ | $\\,51.805\\,$ | $\\,49.244\\,$ | $\\,47.269\\,$ |\n| $41$ | $\\,64.950\\,$ | $\\,56.942\\,$ | $\\,52.949\\,$ | $\\,50.360\\,$ | $\\,48.363\\,$ |\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 49.244)=0.15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=49.244.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
